<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayah State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kayah State is the smallest state of Myanmar, in the hills of the east bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, with its capital at Loikaw. It was formed from the former Karenni States, which joined the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1948, and was renamed Kayah State in 1952. Armed Karenni groups have fought the central government there for much of the period since independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayah-state.jpg" alt="Kayah State" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saosukham · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Htee_Zai_Kha_Waterfall_border_of_Shan_and_Kayar_State_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kayah State, also called Karenni State, lies in eastern Myanmar. It is bounded on the north by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a>, on the east by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>'s Mae Hong Son Province, and on the south and west by Kayin State. Its area is 11,670 square kilometres, and its capital is Loikaw. The relief is mountainous, and the Salween River crosses the state. The Lawpita Dam, built in 1950 under a reparation agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, produces a quarter of the hydroelectric power generated in Myanmar. The Karenni, also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karenni-people">Red Karen</a> or Kayah, a Sino-Tibetan people, form the majority of the population, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Bamar</a>, Shan and Karen minorities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Karenni States were a group of states, among them Kantarawadi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyebogyi">Kyebogyi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bawlake">Bawlake</a>, south of the Federated Shan States and east of British Burma. In 1875 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Burmese king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon Min</a> recognised the area as belonging neither to Burma nor to Britain, so the states were never fully incorporated into British Burma. In 1892 their rulers accepted a stipend and were recognised as tributary. In the 1930s the Mawchi Mine in Bawlake was the most important source of tungsten in the world.</p><p>The 1947 Constitution of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> provided that the three states be amalgamated into one constituent state, Karenni State, with the possibility of secession after ten years. Karenni accounts say that the central government then invaded, and on 9 August 1948 the leader U Bee Htu Re began a resistance, remembered as Karenni Resistance Day. He was assassinated on 8 September 1948 by central government militia, a date remembered as Karenni Martyr's Day. In 1952 the former Shan state of Mong Pai was added and the whole renamed Kayah State, possibly, according to one view, to drive a wedge between the Karenni and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karen-people">Karen people</a> in Karen State, both of whom were fighting for independence.</p><p>In 1957 pro-independence groups formed the Karenni National Progressive Party, backed by its own army, the Karenni Army, which has fought ever since apart from a brief ceasefire in 1995. In 1996 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-law-and-order-restoration-council">State Law and Order Restoration Council</a> stepped up its campaign with a population transfer, forcibly moving villagers to relocation sites to deprive the pro-independence forces of support, and the Myanmar government has been accused of massive human rights violations in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kayah State is the smallest of Myanmar's seven states, with a population of 286,738 in the 2014 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, though it is larger than Yangon Region. The Karenni, Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intha">Intha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Bamar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pao-people">Pa-O</a> live in different parts of the state, and ethnolinguists distinguish many groups. In the 2014 census Buddhists made up 49.9 per cent of the population and Christians 45.8 per cent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kayah State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1948 they joined the Union of Burma, and in 1952 the region became <b>Kayah State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/karenni-states">Karenni States</a></li></ul></section>
